{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** Blood groups are inherited antigens on the red cell surface. In ABO, group A has A antigen, B has B, AB has both and O has neither, and serum holds antibodies against the missing antigen. Rh, mainly the D antigen, is the second key system. Together they decide transfusion compatibility and risk of haemolytic disease of the newborn.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/blood-groups/abo-groups-antigens-antibodies-overview.webp" alt="Diagram of the four ABO blood groups showing the antigens on the red cells and the antibodies in the plasma of each group." caption="Each ABO group has its own red cell antigens and carries antibodies against the antigens it lacks." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1913_ABO_Blood_Groups.jpg" width="960" height="737" /%}

## What is a blood group?

Red cell (erythrocyte) antigens are inherited carbohydrate or protein structures on the outer surface of the red cell membrane. A **blood group** is a defined set of red cell antigens controlled by one genetic locus that has a variable number of alleles. In the ABO system the alleles are A, B and O. The term **blood type** means the antigenic **phenotype**, usually recognised with the help of specific antibodies.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/blood-groups/red-cell-membrane-blood-group-antigens.webp" alt="Diagram of the red cell membrane with blood group antigens projecting from its outer surface." caption="Blood group antigens sit on the outer surface of the red cell membrane." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RBC_membrane_major_proteins.png" width="960" height="614" /%}

These antigens are also called **agglutinogens**, because red cells carrying them clump (agglutinate) when they meet the matching antibody. The matching antibodies are called **agglutinins**.

Hundreds of red cell antigens are known, grouped into many blood group systems. Sources give slightly different counts, but all agree that the **ABO and Rh (Rhesus)** systems matter most clinically. The others become important only when an antibody to them develops.

## The ABO system

The ABO system was discovered by Landsteiner in 1900. He mixed serum from himself and five colleagues with their red cells and saw three patterns of agglutination. The fourth group, AB, was described soon after by his pupils. The discovery made safe blood transfusion possible, and he received the Nobel Prize for it in 1930.

The system rests on two antigens, A and B. They give four groups:

- **Group A:** A antigen present
- **Group B:** B antigen present
- **Group AB:** both A and B present
- **Group O:** neither present

Group A is further divided into subgroups **A1 and A2**. The same subgroups are found in group AB.

### Antigens and antibodies

Each person's serum contains antibodies against the ABO antigen that is **absent** from their own red cells. Group O has both anti-A and anti-B. Group AB has neither.

{% table %}
- **Group**
- **Red cell antigen**
- **Serum antibody**
---
- **A**
- A
- Anti-B
---
- **B**
- B
- Anti-A
---
- **AB**
- A and B
- None
---
- **O**
- None
- Anti-A and anti-B
{% /table %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Rule of thumb:** the antibody is always against the antigen you lack. A group A person has anti-B because they have no B antigen.
{% /callout %}

### Universal donor and universal recipient

- **Group O** red cells carry no A or B antigen, so they are not agglutinated by the recipient's antibodies. Group O is the **universal donor**.
- **Group AB** serum has no anti-A or anti-B, so an AB person can receive red cells of any ABO group. AB is the **universal recipient**.

### Natural and immune antibodies

ABO antibodies are almost absent at birth. They start to appear a few months after birth (the passages give 2 to 8 months, and say they are present by six months). The titre reaches a maximum at 8 to 10 years and then declines slowly through life.

{% table %}
- **Feature**
- **Natural antibodies**
- **Immune antibodies**
---
- **Example**
- Anti-A and anti-B in the ABO system
- Rh antibodies; also anti-A or anti-B after an ABO-incompatible pregnancy or transfusion
---
- **Cause**
- Made in response to environmental antigens in food and bacteria, with no transfusion or pregnancy needed
- Made after a stimulus such as blood transfusion or pregnancy
---
- **Class**
- Usually IgM
- Usually IgG
---
- **Crosses the placenta?**
- No
- Yes
---
- **Behaviour**
- React best between 4 and 18 degrees C, less active at 37 degrees C
- React best at 37 degrees C; act as haemolysins with complement
{% /table %}

Immune antibodies are clinically more important and may cause more severe transfusion reactions.

### Distribution of ABO groups

The frequencies differ between populations. Reported figures for India are:

- **O:** 34 to 40%
- **A:** 22 to 27%
- **B:** 31 to 33%
- **AB:** 5 to 8%

Group O is the commonest and AB the rarest.

## How the ABO antigens are made

A and B antigens are complex oligosaccharides (sugar chains) that differ only in the terminal sugar.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/blood-groups/abo-antigen-biosynthesis-h-antigen.webp" alt="Diagram of the sugar chains of the H, A and B antigens, showing the terminal sugar that distinguishes each one." caption="The H antigen is built first, and the A or B transferase then adds a different terminal sugar to it." credit="Waffeln, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugars_that_form_the_H,_A_and_B_antigens.png" width="803" height="753" /%}

- The **H gene** codes for a fucose transferase that adds a terminal fucose and forms the **H antigen**. H is usually present in people of all ABO groups.
- The **A gene** codes for a transferase that adds N-acetylgalactosamine to H. This makes the A antigen.
- The **B gene** codes for a transferase that adds galactose to H. This makes the B antigen.
- The **O gene** gives an inactive product, so H stays unchanged. Group O red cells therefore carry H but no A or B.
- **Group AB** people have both transferases.

The ABO genes lie on the long arm of chromosome 9. A and B are **codominant**, and O is recessive to both. So AA and AO both give group A, and BB and BO both give group B. Blood group antigens follow simple Mendelian inheritance. For this reason a child's group can differ from that of both parents, if the parents are heterozygous.

### Bombay (Oh) phenotype

Some rare people are homozygous **hh** and cannot make H substance. Without H, no A or B antigen can be built, even if they carry A or B genes. Their red cells test as group O, but their plasma contains **anti-A, anti-B and anti-H**. Ordinary group O people have only anti-A and anti-B.

## The Rh system

The Rh system was first reported by Landsteiner and Weiner in 1940. It is the second most important system in transfusion medicine, because antigen-positive red cells often immunise antigen-negative people through transfusion and pregnancy.

- The **D antigen** is the most important and most common Rh antigen.
- **Rh-positive** people have the D antigen on their red cells and no anti-D in serum.
- **Rh-negative** people lack the antigen. They do not have anti-D unless they have been exposed to it.
- Rh inheritance is controlled by two closely linked genes. One codes for the protein carrying D (RhD). The other codes for the protein carrying C or c and E or e (RhCE).
- Unlike ABO, Rh antigens are found only on red cells, and Rh antibodies are **immune antibodies**. They appear only after a stimulus such as a transfusion or a pregnancy.

A first mismatched transfusion may cause no reaction in an Rh-negative person, but antibodies can form. Later exposure can then cause a reaction. A D-negative person who received D-positive blood even years ago can have appreciable anti-D and react to another D-positive transfusion. In ABO incompatibility the reaction develops immediately.

## Other blood group systems

These systems matter clinically only once an antibody has formed.

{% table %}
- **System**
- **Antibody frequency**
- **Haemolytic transfusion reaction**
- **Haemolytic disease of newborn**
---
- **ABO**
- Almost universal
- Yes (common)
- Yes (usually mild)
---
- **Rh**
- Common
- Yes (common)
- Yes
---
- **Kell**
- Occasional
- Yes (occasional)
- Anaemia, not haemolysis
---
- **Duffy**
- Occasional
- Yes (occasional)
- Yes (occasional)
---
- **Kidd**
- Occasional
- Yes (occasional)
- Yes (occasional)
---
- **Lewis**
- Occasional
- Yes (rare)
- No
{% /table %}

- **Kell:** the K antigen is routinely tested. Kell is the third most immunogenic system after ABO and Rh. In Kell disease of the newborn the infant often has too few circulating reticulocytes because red cell production is suppressed, so the anaemia is hypoproliferative.
- **Duffy:** the Fy(a-b-) phenotype is found almost only in people of African ancestry and is linked to resistance to *Plasmodium vivax* malaria.
- **Lewis:** Lewis antigens are made in plasma and secretions and are taken up by red cells by adsorption. This is why Lewis mismatch rarely causes haemolysis.

## Determining the blood group

The principle is simple. Red cells carry antigens and plasma carries natural antibodies. To find a person's group, their red cells are mixed with sera that contain known antibodies, and we look for agglutination.

**Cell (forward) grouping:** make a 2 to 5% red cell suspension in saline. Add it to anti-A, anti-B and anti-AB sera. Centrifuge and look for agglutination with the naked eye or a microscope.

**Serum (reverse) grouping:** add the unknown serum to known red cells of groups A, B and O, and look for agglutination.

The tube method, which does both, is more accurate than cell grouping alone.

**Rh grouping:** mix the red cell suspension with anti-D serum. Agglutination present means Rh-positive. Agglutination absent means Rh-negative.

Precautions:

- Always run a **control** (cells in saline) to exclude a false positive.
- Dilute the blood. Undiluted blood can give false positives that look like clumping.
- Examine the mixture **before it dries**, and confirm minimal agglutination under the microscope. Be careful to tell true clumping from **rouleaux** (stacking of red cells).

## Compatibility testing and crossmatching

Before a transfusion the following steps are taken:

1. The recipient's **ABO and Rh group** is determined.
2. The recipient's serum is screened for important antibodies by an **indirect antiglobulin (indirect Coombs) test** on a panel of group O red cells.
3. If an antibody is found, donor blood lacking the matching antigen is chosen.
4. A suitable ABO and Rh unit is selected and a **crossmatch** is done.

In the **crossmatch**, the **recipient's serum** is mixed with the **donor's red cells**. If there is no agglutination or haemolysis, the blood is matched. In a mismatch there is agglutination, haemolysis or both.

The full crossmatch has three phases:

- **Immediate phase** (1 to 5 minutes): checks for errors in ABO and Rh typing and picks up ABO incompatibility.
- **Incubation phase** (30 to 45 minutes): detects antibodies mainly of the Rh system.
- **Antiglobulin phase:** detects incomplete antibodies, those that attach to red cells but do not cause agglutination. These include Rh, Kell, Kidd and Duffy antibodies.

The incubation and antiglobulin phases matter because antibodies found there can cause serious haemolytic reactions.

## Storing blood

Citrate prevents clotting by binding calcium. In the preservative used for stored blood, phosphate is a buffer and dextrose is an energy source for the red cells. Adenine lets red cells make ATP again, which extends storage from 21 to 35 days. Storage at 1 to 6 degrees C slows glycolysis. Red cells show 80% survival 24 hours after transfusion if the blood was stored for up to 14 days, and survival falls if it was stored longer.

## Haemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN)

HDN occurs when **IgG antibodies from the mother cross the placenta** and haemolyse the fetal red cells. The mother may be sensitised by a previous pregnancy, previous abortion or previous blood transfusion. It can be due to ABO or Rh incompatibility. ABO incompatibility is much commoner, but the disease is usually mild. Rh-D incompatibility causes the more severe disease.

### Rh-D HDN

An Rh-negative mother is sensitised to Rh-positive fetal cells, usually through bleeding at delivery. In a later pregnancy her anti-D crosses the placenta to an Rh-positive fetus. Sensitisation is more likely if mother and fetus are ABO compatible. The effects range by severity:

- **Severe:** intrauterine death from hydrops fetalis (generalised oedema)
- **Moderate:** a baby born with severe anaemia and jaundice from unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia
- **Mild:** anaemia only, with or without jaundice

When unconjugated bilirubin exceeds 20 mg/dl it can be deposited in the basal ganglia. This is **kernicterus**, and it causes permanent brain damage. Bilirubin rarely enters the adult brain, but in infants with this disease it does, possibly because the blood-brain barrier is more permeable in infancy.

### ABO HDN

{% table %}
- **Feature**
- **ABO HDN**
- **Rh-D HDN**
---
- **Typical mother and baby**
- Mother group O (occasionally B), baby A (sometimes B)
- Mother Rh-negative, baby Rh-positive
---
- **First pregnancy**
- Can occur
- Usually the first pregnancy sensitises the mother, and later pregnancies are affected
---
- **Antiglobulin (Coombs) test**
- Generally negative or weakly positive
- Positive
---
- **Severity**
- Usually mild
- Can be severe
{% /table %}

Why is ABO HDN usually mild?

- Anti-A and anti-B are mostly **IgM**, which does not cross the placenta. Only the immune IgG antibodies of some group O mothers do.
- A and B antigens are **not fully developed** on fetal red cells.
- Maternal IgG is partly neutralised by A and B antigens on other cells and in plasma and tissue fluids.

### Treatment

- **Phototherapy** degrades bilirubin and reduces the chance of kernicterus.
- **Exchange transfusion** is indicated for severe anaemia or a high or rapidly rising bilirubin. The donor blood should be ABO compatible with mother and infant, lack the antigen the maternal antibody is directed against, be fresh (not more than 5 days old), be irradiated and be CMV-negative.
- Antenatal intravenous immunoglobulin to the mother reduces the need for exchange transfusion.

### Prevention of Rh sensitisation

- Every pregnant woman has her ABO and RhD group and red cell antibodies checked at booking (12 to 16 weeks) and again at 28 to 34 weeks.
- **Antenatal anti-D** is offered at 28 to 34 weeks to RhD-negative women with no immune anti-D.
- After delivery of an RhD-positive baby, **anti-D immunoglobulin is given within 72 hours** if the mother is RhD-negative, the baby is RhD-positive and the mother has no anti-D already. A maternal sample is checked for remaining fetal red cells (the Kleihauer test), and more anti-D is given if needed.
- Anti-D is also given after sensitising events such as ectopic pregnancy, threatened miscarriage, termination, abdominal trauma, amniocentesis or chorionic villus biopsy.
- Rh-negative girls and women of childbearing age should receive Rh-matched blood. Group O Rh-negative blood is used in emergencies.
- There is no specific drug to prevent sensitisation to any blood group other than RhD.

## Blood groups and disease

Some groups are statistically linked to disease. The differences are not large.

{% table %}
- **Group or phenotype**
- **Association**
---
- **Group A**
- Cancers of salivary gland, stomach, colon and ovary; thrombosis, because coagulation factors VIII, V and IX are higher
---
- **Group O**
- Duodenal and gastric ulcers, rheumatoid arthritis, von Willebrand disease; malaria is less severe than in non-O patients
---
- **Fy(a-b-)**
- Resistance to *Plasmodium vivax*
---
- **ABH non-secretors**
- Greater susceptibility to *Candida albicans*, *Neisseria meningitidis*, *Streptococcus pneumoniae* and *Haemophilus influenzae*
{% /table %}

Having anti-B may give some protection against *Salmonella*, *Shigella*, *Neisseria gonorrhoeae* and some *E. coli* infections. Some microorganisms carry structures similar to blood group antigens.

## Uses of blood group typing

- Ensuring compatible blood transfusion
- Preventing haemolytic disease of the newborn from Rh incompatibility
- Settling paternity disputes. Blood typing can show that a man **cannot** be the father, but it cannot prove that he is. Typing for other antigens, and DNA fingerprinting, raise the exclusion rate.
- Detecting susceptibility to certain diseases

A person's blood group is determined before any surgery. It is also noted on the identity card.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="A group A person has no B antigen, so their serum contains natural anti-B. These antibodies agglutinate group B red cells at once, so ABO incompatibility causes an immediate reaction." %}
A group A patient is accidentally transfused with group B red cells. The reaction occurs because:
- A. Group A serum contains anti-A
- B. Group A serum contains natural anti-B
- C. Group B red cells carry the D antigen
- D. Group A red cells lack the H antigen
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Anti-A and anti-B are mostly IgM and do not cross the placenta, and A and B antigens are not fully developed on fetal red cells. Only the immune IgG antibodies of some group O mothers cause disease, and it is usually mild." %}
ABO haemolytic disease of the newborn is usually mild mainly because:
- A. Fetal red cells lack the H antigen
- B. The mother is always Rh-positive
- C. Most anti-A and anti-B are IgM and do not cross the placenta, and A and B antigens are poorly developed at birth
- D. Anti-D neutralises the maternal antibodies
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Anti-D is given to an RhD-negative mother within 72 hours of delivering an RhD-positive baby, provided she does not already have anti-D. It prevents her from forming antibodies that could cause HDN in a later pregnancy." %}
Anti-D immunoglobulin should be given within 72 hours of delivery to:
- A. Any mother who has a group O baby
- B. An Rh-positive mother with an Rh-negative baby
- C. An Rh-negative mother with an Rh-negative baby
- D. An Rh-negative mother with an Rh-positive baby, who has no anti-D already
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Bombay (Oh) people lack H substance, so no A or B antigen can be formed. They type as group O but also have anti-H, unlike ordinary group O people." %}
A person's red cells test as group O. Their serum agglutinates ordinary group O red cells. The most likely explanation is:
- A. Bombay phenotype with anti-H in the serum
- B. Group AB with weak antigens
- C. Rh-negative status
- D. A recent transfusion of group A blood
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Rh antibodies are immune antibodies. They develop only after a stimulus such as transfusion or pregnancy. ABO antibodies are natural antibodies and appear without that stimulus." %}
Which statement about Rh antibodies is correct?
- A. They are natural IgM antibodies present from birth
- B. They develop in response to a stimulus such as transfusion or pregnancy
- C. They cannot cross the placenta
- D. They are present in all Rh-negative infants
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The crossmatch mixes the recipient's serum with the donor's red cells. If there is no agglutination or haemolysis, the blood is matched. Agglutination or haemolysis means a mismatch." %}
In a major crossmatch, a compatible result is shown by:
- A. Agglutination of the donor serum with recipient cells
- B. Haemolysis of the recipient's cells
- C. No agglutination or haemolysis when recipient serum is mixed with donor red cells
- D. Rouleaux formation on the slide
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the difference between a blood group and a blood type?" %}
A blood group is a defined set of red cell antigens controlled by one genetic locus with a variable number of alleles, such as A, B and O. A blood type is the antigenic phenotype, usually recognised with the help of specific antibodies.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which antigens and antibodies are present in each ABO group?" %}
Group A has A antigen and anti-B. Group B has B antigen and anti-A. Group AB has both antigens and no antibodies. Group O has no antigen and both anti-A and anti-B.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is group O called the universal donor and AB the universal recipient?" %}
Group O red cells carry no A or B antigen, so the recipient's antibodies do not agglutinate them. Group AB serum has no anti-A or anti-B, so AB people can receive red cells of any ABO group.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Differentiate natural from immune antibodies." %}
Natural antibodies, such as anti-A and anti-B, arise without transfusion or pregnancy, are usually IgM and do not cross the placenta. Immune antibodies, such as Rh antibodies, follow a stimulus such as transfusion or pregnancy, are usually IgG and cross the placenta.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the Bombay phenotype?" %}
The person is homozygous hh and cannot make H substance, so no A or B antigen is formed. The red cells test as group O, but the plasma has anti-A, anti-B and anti-H.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How does ABO HDN differ from Rh HDN?" %}
ABO HDN can occur in the first pregnancy, the antiglobulin test is generally negative, and the disease is less severe. It usually affects a group A or B baby of a group O mother. Rh HDN typically affects later pregnancies and can be severe, with hydrops, anaemia, jaundice or kernicterus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How do you prevent Rh sensitisation?" %}
Give anti-D to RhD-negative pregnant women at 28 to 34 weeks if they have no immune anti-D. After delivery of an RhD-positive baby, give anti-D within 72 hours, and give more if tests show many fetal cells in the mother's blood. Also give it after sensitising events such as miscarriage, ectopic pregnancy or amniocentesis.
{% /viva %}

